issues.problematic_code_snippet:
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions;

namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.Circuits
{
    public class ServerComponentDeserializerTest
    {

var parameters = deserializedDescriptor.Parameters.ToDictionary();
Assert.Single(parameters);
Assert.Contains("Value", parameters.Keys);
Assert.Equal(42, Convert.ToInt64(parameters["Value"]!, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));

var secondDescriptor = descriptors[1];
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericTestComponent<string>).FullName, secondDescriptor.ComponentType.FullName);
Assert.Equal(0, secondDescriptor.Sequence);

var preamble = match.Groups["preamble"].Value;
var preambleMarker = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ComponentMarker>(preamble, ServerComponentSerializationSettings.JsonSerializationOptions);
Assert.NotNull(preambleMarker.PrerenderId);
Assert.Equal("webassembly", preambleMarker.Type);
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<string>).Assembly.GetName().Name, preambleMarker.Assembly);
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<string>).FullName, preambleMarker.TypeName);

findings.body:
`CanPrerender_ClosedGenericComponent_ClientMode` renders `GenericComponent<int>` (line 825) and expects the content `<p>Generic value: 456</p>`, which matches the int render. The preamble assertions, though, compare against `GenericComponent<string>`. The `Assembly` check still passes because both types live in the same assembly, but the `TypeName` check does not. `WebAssemblyComponentSerializer` writes the rendered type's `FullName` into the marker, so `preambleMarker.TypeName` will be the `GenericComponent`1[[System.Int32...` name. The assertion expects the `System.String` name, so the test fails deterministically.

::diff{file="src/Components/Endpoints/test/EndpointHtmlRendererTest.cs" lines="825-838"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The test was copied from the server-mode test, which uses `GenericComponent<string>` for the Server render, and the type argument was not updated when the render used `int`. Switching the expected type to `GenericComponent<int>` fixes the assertion.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Endpoints/test/EndpointHtmlRendererTest.cs" lines="837-838"}
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<int>).Assembly.GetName().Name, preambleMarker.Assembly);
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<int>).FullName, preambleMarker.TypeName);

The diff converts `ServerComponentDeserializerTest.cs` from a file-scoped namespace to a block-scoped namespace and leaves the class body at its old indentation. The class body, which sits under a `public class` at 4 spaces, now has two closing braces at the end of the file, and the braces balance. The file compiles, but it now breaks the repo's file-scoped namespace convention and the body is no longer indented to match the braces. None of this is needed for the new tests, and it makes the diff noisy for reviewers.

